{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 18, :comments ["<b>And that year ended.</b> The year in which he sustained them with bread in exchange for all their livestock — and it is the sixth year." "<b>And they came to him in the second year.</b> This is the seventh year, which is second to the sixth. And they said to him that he should acquire them and their land in exchange for bread, that he would feed them in this seventh year; and after the land belongs to Pharaoh, he would give them seed so that the land not lie fallow — for they know that the seven years of famine are about to end, and it will have seed and harvest. This is what is said: <b>Why should we die before your eyes… Buy us and our land for bread, and we and our land will be slaves to Pharaoh.</b> — that he should acquire the land and the bodies. Thus is the matter of this passage explained according to the plain sense." "<b>And by way of</b> the Midrash, our Rabbis of blessed memory held that there were only two years of famine; one is forced to explain 'and they came to Joseph in the second year' as the second year of the famine, for when Jacob descended to Egypt the famine immediately ceased in his merit. And if you ask: if so, the people of the land would suspect Joseph in his wisdom regarding the interpretation of the dream, for he said there would be seven years of famine and there were only two years — the answer to this is that after his father's death the famine returned to its former state, and with this everything fits and falls into place. The Midrash of our Rabbis of blessed memory is upheld — that there were only two years — and Joseph's interpretation, in which he said there would be seven years of famine, is also proven true, for they were completed afterward, since he did not say there would be seven consecutive years of famine." "<b>And this is</b> what our Rabbis of blessed memory expounded: Rabbi Yosi said, when our father Jacob died the famine returned to its former state. And this is what is written, 'ויברך יעקב את פרעה' — with what did he bless him? That two years of famine were averted; nevertheless they were completed after his death, as it is said (Genesis 45:11): ״וכלכלתי אותך שם״, and it is written after Jacob's death (Genesis 50:21): ״אנכי אכלכל אתכם״ — just as the כלכול mentioned there speaks of years of famine, so the כלכול mentioned here speaks of years of famine. Rabbi Shimon says: this is not a sanctification of the Name, for the words of the righteous are fulfilled in their lifetime and nullified after their death. Rabbi Elazar son of Rabbi Shimon says: I prefer the words of Rabbi Yosi over the words of my father, for this is a sanctification of the Name: as long as the righteous are in the world there is blessing in the world; when they depart from the world, blessing departs from the world."], :book :genesis, :voice :rabbeinu-bahya, :lang :en, :chapter 47, :notes {}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-30T14:21:01.777343043Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}